and that's great.

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.610
And then they often
go to Docker-compose

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.026
when they have
multiple containers

00:02:11.026 --> 00:02:13.390
they want to work with and
have them work together.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:15.730
And a question I see quite
often in the community

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:17.260
is, oh, I've got
this Compose thing,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:18.700
but then I want to
go to Kubernetes.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.200
And I know about Compose, but
I don't know about Kubernetes.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.600
What do I do?

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:23.705
I don't know.

00:02:23.705 --> 00:02:26.080
And so this is actually an
open-source tool that lets you

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.640
take Docker-compose as
well as a Docker DAB file,

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.090
or a Docker Distributed
Application Bundle,

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.910
and then you can convert it
into Kubernetes deployments

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:39.070
and services using
this command line tool,

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:41.820
either to push it
directly up to--

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
Kubernetes Master?

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:43.870
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.610
Or actually just create
the files themselves.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.250
And then you can edit
them as you see fit.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:47.620
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: Cool.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:48.490
That is pretty nice.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:52.360
So basically it is
like a one-off thing.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.690
Like you have a big
Compose file that you

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.310
spend a lot of time building.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.350
And rather than having
two of them manually,

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.340
just use that, and boom, done.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:02.150
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
then you're good to go.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.691
