00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:42.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
Oh, it's less fancy.

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:44.110
It's still pretty cool, though.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:45.320
Nice.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.990
So we've said the
word API-- well,

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:51.920
the letters API-- many,
many times already.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:56.467
Why don't you give us your
definition of what an API is.

00:04:56.467 --> 00:04:57.050
ALAN HO: Yeah.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:02.450
Well, an API is an Application
Programming Interface.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:05.010
But today, I mean, there's
a lot of application.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:07.490
I mean, we've been
having APIs forever,

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.730
but today, when we say APIs,
we usually specifically

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.920
refer to web APIs, web
APIs hosted in the cloud

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.440
or within your own data center.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.800
And so I think there's
nothing-- APIs,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.760
probably the better question
to ask is not what is an API?

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:33.517
But rather, what makes a
good API versus a bad API?

00:05:33.517 --> 00:05:35.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
Then answer that question.

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:36.410
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
what does make

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:37.940
a great API versus a bad API?

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:38.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY
FLORES: We're totally

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.400
happy with that
different question, too.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.280
ALAN HO: Yeah, I think
the primary difference

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.580
is kind of your mindset.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:49.070
And so there's the mindset for
a lot of developers when they

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.570
create APIs, they
think about just

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:53.810
exposing their interface,
their application

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:56.730
through a HTTP interface.

00:05:56.730 --> 00:05:58.400
That's one way to
think about it.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:02.030
But good APIs think
more about the consumer.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:04.400
They think about it
as, what's the easiest

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:08.360
way that a consumer can derive
value from the application

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.380
I built?

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.080
And when I say the
easiest way, that

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:15.090
means there's a lot of things
that are necessary to happen.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.970
It's not just throw
on an interface

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:24.410
and have a PDF to let
you code against it,

00:06:24.410 --> 00:06:26.810
but there's a lot
of different steps

00:06:26.810 --> 00:06:30.620
you need to take
for building an API.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.600
But I want to turn it around.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:37.070
Like how does a GCP
think about APIs?

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.444
What makes a good API
from a GCP standpoint?

00:06:39.444 --> 00:06:41.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
That's a very good question.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.320
I'd say that it's something that
empowers our customers to do

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:49.730
whatever they need in the
easiest and most efficient way.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:52.640
I think that that is
maybe a decent definition.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:53.220
I don't know.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.260
What do you think, Mark?

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:55.620
MARK MANDEL: I think you gave
a very good answer, Francesc.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:57.219
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: OK.

00:06:57.219 --> 00:06:58.510
And that was not even prepared.

00:06:58.510 --> 00:06:59.770
I just nailed that.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:02.570
Yeah, so I think that
what you were pointing out

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:05.840
is it is not just
about giving a way

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.310
to access our services,
because otherwise,

00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:12.996
doing phone calls could
be also an API somehow.

00:07:12.996 --> 00:07:14.450
ALAN HO: That's right.

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:16.241
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
It is really making

00:07:16.241 --> 00:07:17.420
it as efficient as possible.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.795
MARK MANDEL: I think they
should bring back fax machines.

00:07:19.795 --> 00:07:21.755
I think that's what
I'm waiting for.

00:07:21.755 --> 00:07:22.130
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
That's definitely a good idea.

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.171
MARK MANDEL: That's how
I'm going to build my API

00:07:24.171 --> 00:07:25.600
interfaces from now on.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.183
FRANCESC CAMPOY
FLORES: So now that we

00:07:27.183 --> 00:07:29.140
have kind of a good
definition, an idea

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:36.095
of what a good API is,
let's talk about why.

00:07:36.095 --> 00:07:39.020
Why would a company
create an API?

00:07:41.668 --> 00:07:46.970
ALAN HO: There's actually
multiple, I would say,

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:50.872
stages of value that a
company can create in API.

00:07:50.872 --> 00:07:54.980
I mean, so the first
reason is a lot

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.600
of companies are now building
their software, actually,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.690
as micro services.

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:02.960
And all micro services
communicate via API.

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.470
So any company that is
building a micro service today

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:08.770
is essentially building an API.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.690
That's kind of like the lowest
level of value in my opinion

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.480
because it's all
about making sure

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.550
that you create these
connected applications

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:21.890
and let teams not
have to work on one

00:08:21.890 --> 00:08:23.820
big monolithic application.

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.650
So maybe that's
the lowest level.

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.535
The second level of
companies who are making APIs

00:08:28.535 --> 00:08:35.030
is that they want to expose
their software as a product.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:38.330
So basically,
especially if you want

00:08:38.330 --> 00:08:44.150
to create a developer-- if you
have a core value proposition

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:46.340
and you need to
enable developers

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.267
to build on top
of it, they would

00:08:48.267 --> 00:08:49.350
be exposed to the product.

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.340
And the classic
example is mobile apps.

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:55.210
Today, if you have a mobile
app, it talks through an API.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:01.910
It's usually not a web page.

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:04.880
And even it is a web page,
it's usually a single-page app.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.040
So they're calling APIs.

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:09.650
MARK MANDEL: So really, you need
APIs so that you could have,

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:12.080
potentially, a variety
of clients or front

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:14.990
ends to be able to access the
information that your business

00:09:14.990 --> 00:09:16.125
or your service provides.

00:09:16.125 --> 00:09:16.720
Would that be accurate?

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:17.428
ALAN HO: Correct.

00:09:17.428 --> 00:09:18.480
Yeah.

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.150
It sounds simplistic,
but that is the case.

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:25.370
And I think the
highest form of APIs,

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:28.340
though, is more
building an ecosystem.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.730
For example, Walgreens,
a lot of people

00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:34.420
know Walgreens is a place where
they can pick up drugs or print

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:36.140
out pictures.

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:39.830
So what they found is that when
people go pick up a picture,

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:44.330
they end up doing other things
like they buy some chips,

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:48.410
or they might buy a toy, or
do some grocery shopping,

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:49.820
things of that sort.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:52.700
In fact, they found
people who does something

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:56.500
on the web and mobile and
then come into the store

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:00.590
to do something have
almost a 6x increase

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:06.710
in the amount of revenue that
they spend per store visit.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:13.490
So it becomes an advantage to
have as much usage of mobile

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:15.020
prior to going into the store.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:17.590
So what they did is then
they opened up their API

00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:20.050
to all these photo apps.

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:23.210
There's tons of
photo apps out there.

00:10:23.210 --> 00:10:25.040
And what they let
you do is they allow

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:27.500
people to print
their photos straight

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.030
from these photo
apps in the store

00:10:29.030 --> 00:10:30.620
and pick it up in the store.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:32.900
So that way, they drive
a lot of foot traffic

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:35.330
and they have an
ecosystem of applications

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:38.870
that are driving this foot
traffic into the store.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:44.780
So there's multiple levels of
reasons and business reasons

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.040
why you can use APIs.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.180
And it just all matters on
which particular use case

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:51.847
and what drives the
most business value.

00:10:51.847 --> 00:10:53.055
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: Cool.

00:10:53.055 --> 00:10:55.220
So as soon as you start
doing things like this,

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:59.289
though, where you are exposing
your API to the world,

00:10:59.289 --> 00:11:00.830
there's a bunch of
things that you're

00:11:00.830 --> 00:11:03.680
going to need to start handling,
like security, but also

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:04.340
like quotas.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:08.530
And, I mean, someone is going to
need to pay for those pictures.

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:11.410
How do you manage those things?

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:16.700
ALAN HO: Well, as we talk about
application development life

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.940
cycles, in a similar
way, there is

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:22.600
what we call the API lifecycle.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:31.280
And there are probably
eight major steps

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:34.450
of doing development.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:38.690
And if you've got questions,
feel free to interrupt me.

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:43.100
And those steps break
down to design, develop,

00:11:43.100 --> 00:11:46.460
securing your API,
publishing your API,

00:11:46.460 --> 00:11:51.830
scaling it, monitoring it,
analyzing the people who use

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:55.890
the API, and last but not
least, monetizing your API.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:12:01.370
And so for each of
these steps, there's

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:04.130
going to be a set of
other tasks to do.

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:11.540
So for example, when you're
first designing your API,

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.750
what we recommend
is that you start

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:18.200
sharing the design of your
API with your consumers

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:19.304
as quickly as possible.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:26.060
One of the very
popular standard is

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:31.160
Swagger, which has now been
re-branded to Open API.

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:34.226
That's a very, very easy way
and a language-agnostic way

00:12:34.226 --> 00:12:35.975
to actually understand
whether people like

00:12:35.975 --> 00:12:37.950
your API in the first place.

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:41.540
So getting customers to
actually-- having the design,

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:45.020
making sure you have the tools
to circulate that and co-edit

00:12:45.020 --> 00:12:50.160
those APIs, almost like
you're editing a Google Doc,

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:53.510
is going to be very,
very important for making

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:55.902
sure your API is consumable.

00:12:55.902 --> 00:12:57.290
And then the next
step of that is

00:12:57.290 --> 00:12:59.390
that when you're
developing your APIs--

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:02.390
I'm not talking about developing
the application logic,

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:04.800
but your actual APIs--
there's a lot of things

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:06.050
that you also need to do, too.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:07.820
So for example, if
your back end so

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:14.150
happens to be an old
school SOAP service,

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:19.310
you may want to
create an API with out

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:25.070
of the box-- use some way
to convert SOAP to REST.

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:28.070
And especially if you have
more complicated use cases,

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:32.120
you might actually have to
use JavaScript, or Node,

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:38.690
or something of that sort to
facade your existing backend.

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:41.510
Then the next step of that
would be to secure your API.

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:47.990
So we're talking about
having OAuth, SSL, putting

00:13:47.990 --> 00:13:54.260
in some, what I would call,
threat protection for detecting

00:13:54.260 --> 00:13:58.607
SQL injection attacks,
things of that sort.

00:13:58.607 --> 00:14:00.815
One of the things that people
don't realize with APIs

00:14:00.815 --> 00:14:07.340
is that, unlike web pages,
you can leak a lot more data

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:09.160
through an API than a web page.

00:14:12.110 --> 00:14:15.740
And a lot of times when
people develop their APIs,

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:18.740
they try to develop their
APIs for as many consumers as

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:19.880
possible.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:24.680
So they end up returning
lots and lots of information

00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:28.640
through the API, which is
a very, very bad practice.

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:30.800
So making sure your
API is stripping out

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:34.460
things that are sensitive,
things that the customer really

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:38.270
doesn't need becomes very
important in security.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:41.540
And last but not
least, like today we've

00:14:41.540 --> 00:14:43.970
got a lot of denial-of-service
bots out there,

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:49.010
or bots that scrape websites.

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.990
Bots are now getting
smart to the point

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:53.960
that they can scrape
the APIs themselves.

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:58.910
So being able to detect what
we call low and slow attacks,

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:01.490
and then doing things
like a honeypot

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:05.060
so that you can direct
people to a fake API,

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:08.150
a mock API, instead
of your real data,

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:11.092
becomes really important
from a security standpoint.

00:15:11.092 --> 00:15:13.550
MARK MANDEL: So it sounds like
there's a bit of a balancing

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:14.390
act there, too.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:16.100
Because obviously,
if you've got,

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:17.960
say, an ecosystem
that you're building

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.087
and you want people to
come and use your APIs,

00:15:20.087 --> 00:15:22.170
you kind of want to expose
it as much as possible.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:23.480
But on the flip
side, you don't want

00:15:23.480 --> 00:15:25.800
to expose anything that may
be sensitive or harmful.

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.280
How do you kind of
combat that, or at least

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:30.890
find the right level of
the sweet spot of where

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:33.590
that could be?

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:36.260
ALAN HO: So that's where
what I would actually do

00:15:36.260 --> 00:15:39.860
is I would look at the
particular use case.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:46.466
So you might have an API
that might be used in a B

00:15:46.466 --> 00:15:51.080
to B scenario, like a
server calling that API.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:54.080
Or it might be used
directly by a mobile app.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:56.390
In those scenarios, what I
would personally recommend

00:15:56.390 --> 00:16:01.280
is that you split them
into two different APIs.

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:06.980
The one for the mobile app
might have less information,

00:16:06.980 --> 00:16:09.432
and it would be definitely
protected by OAuth.

00:16:09.432 --> 00:16:12.860
And then, the one that's meant
to be called from a server,

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:16.670
it might have a different
authentication authorization

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:19.250
mechanism and serve
more data through it.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:22.550
So my recommendation is that
if you have multiple use

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:25.550
cases that are
very, very different

00:16:25.550 --> 00:16:27.480
that serves different
information,

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:30.770
don't try to jam it
all into one API,

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:33.060
but rather, have multiple APIs.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.900
Now, remember, just because
you have multiple APIs

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:38.930
doesn't mean that you can't
still have one implementation.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.760
It just means creating
multiple facades

00:16:40.760 --> 00:16:42.746
on the same backend system.

00:16:42.746 --> 00:16:43.700
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:44.280
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: Cool.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.500
There's a question that
I've got a couple of times.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.550
And since you're here, I
wonder if you could answer it,

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.590
which is, let's
imagine that you want

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:55.310
to have your API
available to the world

00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:58.640
and you have an interest of
as many people as possible

00:16:58.640 --> 00:17:02.270
using it, but you
have terms of service.

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.670
How do you make sure
that only the people that

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:06.740
have agreed to terms
of services or things

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:10.550
like that-- people that
are not doing something

00:17:10.550 --> 00:17:14.193
that you consider as abuse of
your API-- have access to it?

00:17:14.193 --> 00:17:15.859
What are the kind of
techniques that you

00:17:15.859 --> 00:17:17.420
would recommend for that?

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:20.119
ALAN HO: So I mean, if you
go to most cloud providers,

00:17:20.119 --> 00:17:26.735
they basically have different
quota limits for each user.

00:17:28.954 --> 00:17:30.620
If you want to expose
this to the world,

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:33.290
the number one thing to do is
create a portal, a developer

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:39.050
portal where people can, in a
self-service way, get API keys.

00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:41.570
And when they sign up
for these APIs keys,

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:44.510
you can assign
different quota limits

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:47.640
for each of the--
depending on what service

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.760
they're signing up for.

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:55.040
And then within a gateway--
this is the purpose

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:58.700
of why you would want to have a
gateway in front of your APIs.

00:17:58.700 --> 00:18:01.550
The gateway would be able to
do counting and making sure

00:18:01.550 --> 00:18:05.260
that people don't exceed
their particular limits.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:06.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: Cool.

00:18:06.710 --> 00:18:09.260
So I think I interrupted
you in the middle of the API

00:18:09.260 --> 00:18:09.950
lifecycle.

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:13.020
So we were talking about
how to secure an API.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:15.527
What could be the next step?

00:18:15.527 --> 00:18:16.110
ALAN HO: Yeah.

00:18:16.110 --> 00:18:18.140
So after you secure
your API, you

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:21.570
would publish it so that
the world can see it.

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:25.880
And publishing an API,
a lot of times people

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:28.490
say, oh, I just need to publish
the API in developer portal

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:30.170
if I have a public API.

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.660
Well, the truth is,
even if your API is only

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:35.210
being used within
your own company,

00:18:35.210 --> 00:18:37.640
it's probably a good
idea to publish it

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:39.830
so that people within
your own company

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:42.800
are not reinventing the wheel.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:44.600
It's also really
important to have

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:50.000
some sort of issue developer
keys even within your own org

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:54.800
so that you know who within
the company is using your APIs.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:57.260
And it allows you to
communicate to those people

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:00.860
when the APIs are going
to change or deprecated.

00:19:00.860 --> 00:19:04.220
And last but not least, you need
that for chargebacks as well.

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.950
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so that
sounds interesting as well.

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:09.740
Now, I don't know a huge amount
of Swagger about Open API,

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:11.420
but does that help
with that process

00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:13.730
of being able to communicate
about what's published

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:16.400
and what's not published
and how those things work?

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:19.880
ALAN HO: Yeah, so the
great thing about Swagger

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:24.620
is that after you define your
model and you define your APIs,

00:19:24.620 --> 00:19:26.810
you can actually annotate
your Swagger document

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:30.920
and put the documentation
within Swagger itself.

00:19:30.920 --> 00:19:32.510
And what it'll do
is that you can

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:38.060
use Swagger editors to
generate the documentation

00:19:38.060 --> 00:19:41.240
like live interactive
documentation.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:41.990
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:19:41.990 --> 00:19:44.770
ALAN HO: So instead of just
having like a PDF file,

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:53.810
you can actually create a
live, testable UI that you

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:57.410
can actually, essentially,
do curl commands

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.240
and it would give you
the actual results.

00:19:59.240 --> 00:20:02.390
So after publishing, then
the next thing you need to do

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.550
is scale your API.

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:09.200
And there's multiples from
a scalability standpoint.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:12.050
If your API is being called
from many, many clients

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:14.960
around the world,
you need to make sure

00:20:14.960 --> 00:20:20.780
that you have almost like
a CDN-- you have points

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:23.510
of presence in many,
many different data

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:27.020
centers all around
the world, especially

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:31.820
as close as possible to
the consuming client.

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:37.460
So Apigee, depending on
what kind of service--

00:20:37.460 --> 00:20:41.530
use the on-prime version
or the cloud version--

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:45.810
we're geo-distributed
in lots of data centers.

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:49.300
And we're going to
also be moving on--

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:51.830
and our main one that we
use is Amazon Web Services--

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:53.690
but in the future,
we're obviously

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:56.510
going to be doing Google
Cloud and other cloud platform

00:20:56.510 --> 00:20:58.790
providers out there, too.

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:02.150
The other thing, too, is that
from a scaling standpoint,

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:03.950
it's also really
important to remember

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:08.900
that you can put things like
caching in your gateway, which

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:11.390
will reduce a lot
of the API calls

00:21:11.390 --> 00:21:13.550
to your very backend systems.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:15.480
And that could drastically
reduce the costs

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:18.022
that you need to
scale your system.

00:21:18.022 --> 00:21:19.567
MARK MANDEL: That makes sense.

00:21:19.567 --> 00:21:21.650
ALAN HO: And then, after
you've scaled the system,

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:23.330
the next thing is you've got
to make sure the thing doesn't

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:23.960
fall over.

00:21:23.960 --> 00:21:28.460
So having all the dashboards--
application performance

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:31.340
monitoring dashboards
and logging dashboards--

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:32.792
becomes really important.

00:21:32.792 --> 00:21:34.250
And one of the
things I tell people

00:21:34.250 --> 00:21:38.630
is that, yes, you might
have logging and application

00:21:38.630 --> 00:21:40.830
performance monitoring
on your app,

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:46.220
but if you have monitoring
and logging at the application

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:50.450
interface, at the API
layer, it actually gives you

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.000
a much better sense of what
the customer is actually

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:54.230
experiencing.

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.560
So having monitoring
at that layer,

00:21:57.560 --> 00:22:00.350
not at the application layer,
but the programming interface

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:03.080
layer, it helps you
quickly diagnose

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:12.079
whether your customers are
meeting their SLAs, things

00:22:12.079 --> 00:22:12.620
of that sort.

00:22:12.620 --> 00:22:15.650
Because your application
is somewhat--

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:19.790
it doesn't understand which
particular users are using

00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:25.370
that API, nor does it
understand the level of the SLA

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:27.390
that they have
promised the user.

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:29.970
But at the API tier, it
does understand that.

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.520
So that's where it
becomes very valuable.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:34.940
And then two more
steps, just two more.

00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:39.410
After you make sure
that your API is stable

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:41.690
and it's meeting its
SLAs, then you've

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:44.210
got to figure out who's
actually using your API.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:48.380
So when you're
creating these APIs,

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:51.150
it's almost like a funnel.

00:22:51.150 --> 00:22:55.850
A lot of times developers
would sign up for the API,

00:22:55.850 --> 00:22:59.300
and then maybe
30% or 40% of them

00:22:59.300 --> 00:23:01.490
might actually send an API call.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:04.340
And then, of that,
another maybe 10%

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:10.340
might actually use your
API on a regular basis.

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:13.310
And just understanding those
steps become very important

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:18.620
because as developers
are going down

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:21.140
this, what I call,
developer funnel,

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:23.610
you may want to engage
them in a different way.

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:26.900
So for the people who
have used your API,

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:30.020
there's actually kind of
a magic cut off point.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:33.900
We found that developers use
your APIs for three weeks,

00:23:33.900 --> 00:23:36.110
and maybe not sending
traffic every day,

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:39.380
but they've used your
API for three weeks,

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:42.070
you see that the engagement of
that user and the propensity

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:45.320
for them to buy the
product is way, way higher.

00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:48.230
So kind of understanding
who's using your APIs

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:51.152
and targeting them in a right
way becomes really important.

00:23:51.152 --> 00:23:52.610
MARK MANDEL: Just
a quick question,

00:23:52.610 --> 00:23:54.740
when you say who's
using those APIs,

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:57.020
do you mean specifically
which applications

00:23:57.020 --> 00:23:59.060
are using those APIs
or actually which

00:23:59.060 --> 00:24:01.490
possible customers
through those applications

00:24:01.490 --> 00:24:02.934
are using those APIs?

00:24:02.934 --> 00:24:04.360
ALAN HO: Both.

00:24:04.360 --> 00:24:08.590
Yeah, so you may want to
see, are iOS applications

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:13.310
using me more or are Android
applications using me more?

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:15.860
You might want to
segment your applications

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:18.260
by the type of app.

00:24:18.260 --> 00:24:22.790
But then you might
want to slice and dice

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:25.770
between the types of
companies that are using it.

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:33.489
So is it a startup developer
or is it a large enterprise?

00:24:33.489 --> 00:24:35.780
Like a large enterprise,
they're very likely to pay you

00:24:35.780 --> 00:24:37.640
a lot more money.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:39.866
But if it's a startup
developer for a hot startup,

00:24:39.866 --> 00:24:41.240
you still may want
to engage them

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:44.150
because they could provide
you a lot of great free press

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:46.210
for your APIs.

00:24:46.210 --> 00:24:49.940
So understanding who
is using your API

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:52.130
becomes critically
important for learning

00:24:52.130 --> 00:24:54.000
how to engage those customers.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:54.750
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:24:54.750 --> 00:24:55.958
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: Cool.

00:24:55.958 --> 00:24:58.850
So I think you mentioned
there was a one last.

00:24:58.850 --> 00:25:01.260
ALAN HO: Yeah, one last one
is you've got to make money.

00:25:01.260 --> 00:25:02.600
MARK MANDEL: Profit!

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:03.620
ALAN HO: Right, profit.

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:06.350
Exactly!

00:25:06.350 --> 00:25:09.050
So being able to
charge people's credit

00:25:09.050 --> 00:25:12.710
cards for-- the simple way of
thinking about monetization

00:25:12.710 --> 00:25:14.930
is the Amazon Web Services way.

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:17.480
You charge per API call.

00:25:17.480 --> 00:25:20.030
But the reality is, when
you talk about these API

00:25:20.030 --> 00:25:23.390
ecosystems, for
example, Walgreens,

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:29.150
they don't charge for their API
at all because they want people

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:34.290
to use their API so that more
people come into the store

00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:35.830
and spend more money.

00:25:35.830 --> 00:25:40.200
So instead, they might have
internal charge-back mechanisms

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:43.290
to charge other business
divisions whenever

00:25:43.290 --> 00:25:45.086
their APIs are being used.

00:25:45.086 --> 00:25:48.690
So when you think
about monetization,

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:53.670
you need to think about directly
monetizing it, indirectly

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:56.730
monetizing it, or
internal charge-backs

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:58.050
within your organization.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:25:59.280
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:25:59.280 --> 00:26:01.339
ALAN HO: So, yeah,
that's the life cycle.

00:26:01.339 --> 00:26:02.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY
FLORES: So that seems

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.370
like there's a lot of
things that you should

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:07.260
take into account
whenever you are going

00:26:07.260 --> 00:26:13.470
to publish an API, which I guess
that's the reason why Apigee

00:26:13.470 --> 00:26:16.660
exists at all.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:18.870
So I was wondering
if you could give us

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:24.990
a pretty high view of how does
it look to go through this API

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:28.697
lifecycle using Apigee?

00:26:28.697 --> 00:26:29.280
ALAN HO: Yeah.

00:26:29.280 --> 00:26:36.180
So Apigee is an API management
platform that-- I mean,

00:26:36.180 --> 00:26:38.940
our goal is to make
it very fast for you

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:40.770
to get through this lifecycle.

00:26:40.770 --> 00:26:46.740
Because I just rattled off
a ton of steps, but the way

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:50.580
that we do it is that
we provide design tools.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:56.070
So we have a Swagger editor--
it's a Swagger/Open API

00:26:56.070 --> 00:26:59.310
editor that we've contributed
from an open-source standpoint,

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.430
but we also have it
built into our product.

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:04.830
So we encourage people to
create their API design,

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:08.700
then load their APIs,
then proxy their APIs

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:11.760
through Apigee's gateway.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:13.860
And that provides
you the ability

00:27:13.860 --> 00:27:19.320
to add security, monitoring,
analysis on top of it.

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:24.200
And then we allow people to
publish developer portals

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:26.550
in a turnkey fashion
so that you don't

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:31.680
have to mess around with
Drupal or your own CMS system.

00:27:31.680 --> 00:27:35.050
And then we have out-of-box
connectors for monetization.

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:41.040
So whether it's payment
providers, things of that sort,

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:43.380
we just accelerate
it and we provide

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:46.110
all the connectors for you
to make it stand up your API

00:27:46.110 --> 00:27:48.145
program as quickly as possible.

00:27:48.145 --> 00:27:49.020
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:50.790
I think we'll
definitely get you back

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:53.760
to do a more in-depth analysis
of exactly what Apigee

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:55.120
can do in the API space.

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:57.780
