00:31:35.165 --> 00:31:37.400
And the question is,
OK, so let's imagine

00:31:37.400 --> 00:31:39.470
that I'm building
this application

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:42.240
and I want to use
some cloud provider.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:48.650
So let's say that I'm currently
using storage, so S3 on Amazon.

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:51.470
And I'm considering
moving to Google Cloud

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:52.910
Storage at some point.

00:31:52.910 --> 00:31:55.010
Is there any way that
I can write my code

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:58.340
against an API or
a library that will

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:01.740
abstract the detail of
what is the cloud provider?

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:03.850
So then, to my question,
would it be easier?

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:05.933
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so I
think this is really cool.

00:32:05.933 --> 00:32:09.140
So actually, Aja, who was
on the episode last week,

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:11.390
does a Cloud Minute video
on covering exactly this,

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:12.855
with storage specifically.

00:32:12.855 --> 00:32:14.480
But there's a great
Ruby library called

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:17.450
Fog that literally does exactly
what you're talking about.

00:32:17.450 --> 00:32:21.050
It acts as an abstraction to a
variety, quite a wide variety

00:32:21.050 --> 00:32:24.482
of cloud providers, through
compute, DNS storage,

00:32:24.482 --> 00:32:25.940
and several other
services that are

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:28.332
commonly available
across cloud providers.

00:32:28.332 --> 00:32:30.290
And what makes this
really, really powerful is,

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:34.100
yeah, I could be running on,
say, AWS interacting with S3.

00:32:34.100 --> 00:32:35.264
I work against the API.

00:32:35.264 --> 00:32:36.680
And then if I want
to be like, oh,

00:32:36.680 --> 00:32:38.940
let's see how this works
on Google Cloud storage--

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:39.690
maybe it's better.

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:40.398
Maybe it's worse.

00:32:40.398 --> 00:32:42.110
Let's find out--
all I need to do

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:45.830
is basically change my provider
with my authentication details,

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:47.210
credentials, et cetera.

00:32:47.210 --> 00:32:48.210
And then I'm good to go.

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:49.501
I don't have to change my code.

00:32:49.501 --> 00:32:51.590
I can just push stuff up
into Google Cloud storage

00:32:51.590 --> 00:32:53.300
or whatever it is
I'm working with.

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:55.134
And then I can keep on
moving on from there.

00:32:55.134 --> 00:32:56.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES:
